  [1mThe fork:[0m this is the [3mstored format[0m — the record that will already be sitting in real browsers by the time you want to
  change it. The interface can be revised cheaply; the shape of what's on disk cannot, because old records outlive the
  code that wrote them.

  - [1mAcross restarts, with an expiry stamp[0m — [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m, storing [38;5;153m{ version, userId, username, expiresAt }[39m; reads treat
    an expired record as logged out. Buys a session that ends on its own and a record you can evolve (the [38;5;153mversion[39m field
    is what lets a future change migrate instead of guess). Costs one timestamp check on read and picking a duration
    now.
  - [1mAcross restarts, no expiry[0m — [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m holding just the ID. Simplest thing that matches "persist." Costs the one
    thing that's genuinely hard to undo: adding expiry later means every already-stored record has no timestamp, so you
    either log everyone out at once or special-case the old shape forever.
  - [1mPer tab only[0m — [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m, cleared when the tab closes. Buys the tightest exposure window. But it contradicts
    "persist," and a second tab is a [3mdifferent[0m session, which usually reads as a bug.

  [1mMy recommendation: across restarts with an expiry stamp.[0m You asked for persistence, so per-tab is out. Between the
  other two, the versioned record with [38;5;153mexpiresAt[39m costs a few lines today and is the only one that leaves you room to
  change your mind — which matters precisely because there's no backend yet to correct a bad client-side decision. On
  duration I'd suggest 7 days as a starting point: long enough that people don't notice it, short enough that a shared
  or stolen laptop isn't indefinitely "logged in" as someone.

  Worth restating here: this record is plain text in the browser and any user can edit it to say whatever they want.
  It's fine for prefilling forms and labeling logs; when you do have a backend, authorization must come from a
  server-verified token, never from this.
